# LINE ENDINGS ARE LF IN THE WORKING TREE, ON EVERY MACHINE.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, measured on the Razer 2026-08-31. This repo was written on a Mac and the
# Windows checkout arrived i/lf w/crlf -- LF in the index, CRLF on disk, which is git's default
# there. Every gate that splits a file on "\n" and compares the result to a literal then sees a
# trailing \r, and reports something false with total confidence:
#
#   * org_vendor_check called 20 vendored files "DELETED since vendoring" AND, in the same run,
#     called those same files unknown to the manifest -- a contradiction only \r can produce.
#   * scripts/lib/vision.mjs threw outright, because /```accepted\n/ cannot match "```accepted\r\n",
#     which took the whole `npm test` chain down with it.
#
# Neither was a bug in the game or in the gate's reasoning. Both were the file arriving in a shape
# the author never saw. That is the same family as the em-dash that stopped watchdog.ps1 parsing
# and the bare "C:\" path the ESM loader rejects -- all found the same day, all invisible on macOS.
#
# ONE LINE, not a \r? sprinkled through every parser: a hand-kept list of places to tolerate \r
# rots exactly like the thing it guards, and the next parser somebody writes will not be on it.
#
# NOTE FOR WHOEVER PULLS THIS ONTO A WINDOWS CHECKOUT: this takes effect on CHECKOUT, so an
# existing tree stays CRLF until it is rewritten. Once you are sure nothing uncommitted is at
# risk (`git status` clean), run ALL FOUR of these, in order:
#
#     git config core.autocrlf false
#     git ls-files -z | xargs -0 rm -f
#     git checkout-index -a -f
#     git add --renormalize .
#
# THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS NOTE SAID ONLY `git checkout-index -a -f`, AND THAT COMMAND DOES
# NOTHING AT ALL HERE -- it exits 0, prints nothing, and leaves every CRLF file exactly as it
# was. Measured on the Razer 2026-08-31: 1707 CRLF files before, 1707 after. Three separate
# reasons, each of which alone is enough to make the one-liner a silent no-op:
#
#   1. core.autocrlf=true was set locally and OVERRODE eol=lf on checkout, so even the files
#      git did rewrite came back CRLF. This is the one that makes the failure total.
#   2. checkout-index -f will not overwrite a file its stat cache calls clean, so it skipped
#      nearly everything. Deleting the files first is what forces the rewrite. (`reset --hard`
#      does not help either, and for a subtler reason: `text=auto` normalises CRLF away when
#      git hashes the working-tree file, so GIT CANNOT SEE THE DIFFERENCE IT IS MEANT TO FIX
#      -- every file compares equal to the index and is skipped.)
#   3. Afterwards the index holds stat data from before the rewrite, so `git status` reports
#      ~1704 files modified whose content hashes MATCH the index exactly. `update-index
#      --refresh` will not clear it; `git add --renormalize .` does.
#
# THE LESSON IS THE REPO'S OWN, AGAIN: a command written into a doc and never run is a
# command that does not work. This one had been written, read, and cited in a ledger entry
# as the remaining step, and no one had executed it.
* text=auto eol=lf
